Keep one ticket per evidenced cause, with a count of unique reports. A matching subject helps
find a candidate ticket; subject alone is not a cause.

<Callout>
This guidance follows <a href="/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause">proposed decision 0002</a>,
which Austin must review. <a href="/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject">Accepted decision 0001</a>
is preserved as history; this proposal does not record owner approval.
</Callout>

## The proposed rule

1. Read the body and identify the tenant, its locale, booking form and report channel.
2. Compare that evidence with the candidate ticket's cause. For #4471, a day-first tenant's
   `booking-v3` form reports belong to `hint`; app, email and `booking-v2` form reports belong
   to `parser`. Intake's browser-based tag is a guess, not the tenant's cause.
3. Merge only when both reports have the same known cause, as `sameCause` checks in `causes.ts`.
   Different subjects or tenants can still share a cause. The same subject or tenant can cover
   different causes when the form or channel differs.
4. Keep different causes apart. If a known cause has no ticket, open one. Count each report ID once.

## Try it

<DedupeExplorer />

## When you are unsure

Keep the report separate for review. Record missing tenant, locale, form or channel evidence,
or a body that does not fit the known explanation. An unknown cause is not a match, including
when both reports are unknown. Do not merge on subject or intake tag to clear the queue.

Keep observations attached to their report IDs without inventing a new cause. The one-day
observations in [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471-split) remain included in the parser count; their
root cause and the parser fix's effect on them are unverified.
